menu "LOCK_HYBRID_AI Edge Node Bridge"

    config LOCK_HYBRID_WIFI_SSID
        string "WiFi SSID"
        default "changeme"
        help
            SSID of the site's WiFi network (ASM-1).

    config LOCK_HYBRID_WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "WiFi password"
        default "changeme"
        help
            WPA2-PSK password. Stored in plaintext in sdkconfig like any
            other ESP-IDF project - fine for a dev build, but review
            before shipping a device (flash encryption / NVS-based
            provisioning would be the real fix, neither is set up here).

    config LOCK_HYBRID_BACKEND_BASE_URL
        string "Cloud Backend base URL"
        default "http://192.168.1.100:8000"
        help
            e.g. http://<server-ip>:8000 for the CLOUD_SERVER dev
            instance, or an https:// URL once a reverse proxy with a real
            certificate is in front of it (CI-1 requires encryption in
            transit - this dev default does not provide that).

    config LOCK_HYBRID_DEVICE_API_KEY
        string "Device API key"
        default ""
        help
            The raw key shown once by POST /admin/api/devices in the
            admin web app (CLOUD_SERVER) when this device was registered.
            Cannot be recovered from the server afterwards - re-register
            the device if it's lost.

    config LOCK_HYBRID_BACKEND_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "Backend HTTP request timeout (ms)"
        default 8000
        help
            Generous relative to PR-1's 2s device-to-unlock target - the
            MCXN947 side's own NET_RequestDecision() timeout is what
            actually governs the user-facing latency budget; this is a
            backstop so a hung TCP connection doesn't wedge the worker
            task forever.

    config LOCK_HYBRID_BACKEND_SKIP_TLS_VERIFY
        bool "Skip TLS certificate verification (dev only)"
        default n
        help
            Only for testing against a self-signed cert during
            development. Leave OFF for anything resembling a real
            deployment - CI-1 means "actually encrypted and verified",
            not just "the bytes happen to be TLS".

endmenu
